   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to a license\nto sell liquor at retail for consumption on certain premises",
    "basePrintNo": "S8941",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8941-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §106, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8941\n\nSPONSOR: PALUMBO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to a\nlicense to sell liquor at retail for consumption on certain premises\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo add a parcel of land to the list of premises which are exempt from\nthe provisions of law which generally restrict\nmanufacturers/wholesalers/importers and retailers from sharing an inter-\nest in a liquor license.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Amends subparagraph (xvii) of paragraph (a) subdivision 13 of\nSection 106 of the Alcoholic beverage Control Law by adding a new\nsubparagraph (xviii) to add a parcel of land to the list of premises\nwhich are exempt from the provisions of law which generally restrict\nmanufacturers/wholesalers/importers and retailers from sharing an inter-\nest in a liquor license.\n\nSection 2. Immediate effective date\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nABC Law Section 106(13) prohibits retail licensees from having an inter-\nest in any premises where alcohol is sold by a manufacturer, wholesaler,\nor importer.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThrough what are known as the \"tied-house\" laws -- New York State\nrestricts holders of alcohol manufacturing licenses from having an\ninterest in a retail license. The law is occasionally amended to exempt\ncertain properties from this restriction as this bill would do, when\nthere does not appear to be significant risk of undue market influence.\n\nThe site defined by metes & bounds in this legislation is the Marram\nHotel in Montauk, town of East Hampton, New York. The need for this\nexemption is that one of the three principals of the incoming owners of\nMarram has a financial interest in a small, family winery in California.\nAs New York's \"tied-house\" laws have no de minimis exception, the enact-\nment of this legislation is necessary to allow this principal to be on a\nNew York State retail license.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2022: New Legislation\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nMinimal\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis legislation will take effect immediately.\n",
